{
  "dom_id": "step-53338",
  "name": "Using the Reciprocal",
  "problem": "When solving equations with fractional coefficients, there is a shortcut! We can complete division and multiplication in a single step by multiplying both sides of the equation by the reciprocal of the fractional coefficient.\n\nFor example, to solve the equation $\\dfrac{2x}{5}= 6,$ we first express the fraction as a coefficient:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6\n\t}\n$$\n\nThe fractional coefficient is $\\dfrac{2}{5}$, and its reciprocal is $\\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right)^{-1} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}.$ Because the product of a fraction and its reciprocal is equal to $1,$ we can multiply both sides of the equation by the reciprocal to isolate $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{2}{5}x &= 6 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}}  \\cdot \\dfrac{2}{5} }_{\\large =\\, 1} x &= {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac 5 2}} \\cdot 6 \\[5pt]   \n\t\t1 x  &= \\dfrac{5\\cdot 6}{2} \\[5pt]   \n\t\tx  &= \\dfrac{30}{2} \\[5pt]   \n\n\t\tx &= 15\n\t}\n$$",
